    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/873/sf/en-us/job/561762/assistant-professor-mild-to-moderate-support-needs-special-education": {
      "title": "San Francisco State University - Details - Assistant Professor, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Special Education",
      "description": "San Francisco State University - Details - Assistant Professor, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Special Education Skip to main content SAN FRANCISCO STATE UNIVERSITY | Human Resources A–Z Calendar Login Search\n                                        SF State Search SF State Button SF State This Site Toggle navigation Menu Main navigation What's New Get Started Expand Future Employees New Employees Current Employees HR Liaisons Managers Students Jobs Benefits & Retirement Expand Overview Medical Benefits Dental Benefits Vision Benefits Retirement Services Employee Assistance Program Fee Waiver Program Voluntary Benefits & Discounts Payroll Expand Overview Payroll & Paycheck Essentials Payroll Calendar & Schedules Employment Verification Leave Accrual and Absence Management Resources Cal Employee Connect Employee Separation Clearance Process Classification & Compensation Learning & Development Expand Professional Development Performance Management Process CSU Learn Labor Relations Compliance Expand Overview Whistleblower Leaves/ Employee Accommodations/Workers' Comp Expand Leaves Program Employee Accommodations Services Workers' Compensation Resources Expand COVID-19 Employee Life Events Employee Resource Groups HR Events / Training Staff Years of Service Recognition Workplace Resources Immigration Resources CSU International Travel Expand CSU International Travel Resources CSU International Travel Guidance for Spring & Summer 2025 Community Expectations ADA: 504 and Title II Policies and Procedures Expand Policies & Procedures (Main) All Employees Faculty Management Personnel & Confidential Staff (Represented / Non-Represented) Student Employees Job Opportunities Applicant Login Job Search Filter Results Work type Instructional Faculty – Tenured/Tenure-Track 1 Locations Northern California San Francisco 1 Categories Appointment Type Tenured/Tenure-Track 1 Bargaining Unit Unit 3 - CFA - California Faculty Association 1 Job Search Category/Discipline Faculty - Education/Library Science 1 Time Basis Full Time 1 Workplace Type (Exclude Inst Fac) On-site (work in-person at business location) 1 Assistant Professor, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Special Education Apply now Job no: 561762 Work type: Instructional Faculty – Tenured/Tenure-Track Location: San Francisco Categories: Unit 3 - CFA - California Faculty Association, Tenured/Tenure-Track, Full Time, Faculty - Education/Library Science, On-site (work in-person at business location) Position Description San Francisco State University, Department of Special Education offers an exciting opportunity for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Mild to Moderate Support Needs (MMSN) beginning August 2027. We seek a colleague whose teaching and research interests include expertise in supporting individuals diagnosed with disabilities that require mild to moderate support needs. We are especially interested in qualified candidates with a demonstrated commitment -- through their research, teaching, and service -- to the diversity and excellence of our academic community and department Required qualifications: Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Special Education or a closely related field from an accredited program. Terminal degree must be completed by the first day of employment. Record of successful teaching, preferably in public schools, that includes students with disabilities. Demonstrated ability to incorporate inclusion and educational equity in teaching, and/or scholarship. Record of working and communicating effectively with colleagues and students About San Francisco State University SFSU is a major public urban university, situated in one of the world’s great cities. Building on a century-long history of commitment to quality teaching and broad access to undergraduate and graduate education, the university offers comprehensive, rigorous, and integrated academic programs that require students to engage in open-minded inquiry and reflection. SFSU prepares its students to become productive, ethical, active citizens with a global perspective. Learn more about working at the California State University and the impact of our mission. Mission Statement SFSU educates and equips students to thrive in a global society. We deliver academic excellence by pursuing knowledge, inspiring creativity, supporting our diverse community and advancing social justice and positive change in the world. Visit the Strategic Planning website to learn more . About the Graduate College of Education The Graduate College of Education has close to 1,000 students and serves the San Francsico peninsula and Bay Area, providing teachers in every subject and many specializations. Our Statement of Purpose is that \"The GCOE develops transformative and visionary educators, clinicians, and leaders for social justice, to effect change for good across the Bay Area and beyond, and to create an engaged, and productive democracy. Together we do the work necessary to understand and welcome all; prepare equity-focused, caring, and highly skilled professionals; to identify and dismantle racist, ableist, and oppressive systems; and to build an equitable and accessible present and future.” About the Department of Special Education The Department of Special Education within the Graduate College of Education at SF State is one of the most comprehensive departments of special education in California. We serve students at the undergraduate, post baccalaureate credential, masters, and doctoral levels. We offer an Undergraduate Minor in Special Education, an Undergraduate Minor in Deaf Studies and American Sign Language, four SF State Scholars programs, an Integrated Teacher Education Pathway (ITEP), five stand-alone credential programs including Early Childhood Special Education, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Extensive Support Needs, Visual Impairment and Orientation & Mobility, an alternative intern credential pathway, Masters in Special Education, and a Ph.D. in Special Education (joint doctoral program with UC Berkeley). At the heart of the work in the Department of Specia Education is a commitment to inclusion, belonging, and equity for disabled individuals in schools, communities, and society. We believe every person has the right to be recognized and valued as a full member of our communities, honoring the diverse and intersecting identities we all hold. Through research, teaching, and service, we strive to ensure that special education supports and services are individualized, asset-based, culturally sustaining, collaborative, and interdisciplinary. Responsibilities: The position requires graduate, credential and undergraduate teaching in the area of mild to moderate support needs; mentoring and advising graduate, credential and undergraduate students; developing an active ongoing scholarship program in one’s area of specialty; and ongoing committee and service assignments in the department, college and university. Compensation: The Assistant Professor (Academic Year) classification salary range is: $97,000 to $99,000 annually (12 monthly payments per academic year). Salary offered is commensurate with experience and qualifications. In addition, the California Equal Pay Act prohibits employers from paying its employees less than those of the opposite sex and/or another race or ethnicity for substantially similar work ( https://www.dir.ca.gov/dlse/california_equal_pay_act.htm ). Professional development funds, moving expenses, start‐up funds, and a reduced teaching load may be available. The California State University (CSU) provides generous health, retirement, and other benefits. Application Procedure A complete online application must be submitted to receive consideration. Application review begins October 15, 2026 and continues until position is filled. To apply, please to begin the application process and upload the following materials: to begin the application process and upload the following materials: 1. Cover letter relating your experience to the required qualifications 2. Curriculum vitae 3. A teaching philosophy statement that is no more than 500 words that provides a clear summary of how you approach teacher education in support of SFSU’s diverse student and community populations. As part of this, provide specific examples of how you center disability, inclusion and equity in your teaching. 4. Up to three samples of recent scholarly publications, conference publications, and/or other scholarly work that represent expertise and potential as a researcher 5. Names and contact information of three references who will provide letters of recommendation upon request. Please direct all questions about the position to Denise Miyajima, Department of Special Education Department Coordinator at dmiya@sfsu.edu SFSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and does not discriminate against persons on the basis of race, religion, color, ancestry, age, disability, genetic information, gender, gender identity, gender expression, marital status, medical condition, national origin, sex, sexual orientation, covered veteran status, or any other protected status. We warmly welcome applications from individuals with disabilities, veterans, and others from historically underrepresented groups, who bring diverse experiences and perspectives that strengthen our academic community.  We believe that such diversity enhances our curriculum, research, and student support.  Our campus actively supports reasonable accommodations and works collaboratively with disabled faculty to remove barriers and foster success. The California State University is a state entity whose business operations reside within the State of California.  Because of this, CSU prohibits hiring employees to perform CSU-related work outside of California with very limited exception.  While this position may be eligible for occasional telework, all work is expected to be performed in the state of California, and this position is assigned to on-campus operations. Pursuant to Education Code Section 89521, the CSU is required to contact the current and/or former employer(s) of an applicant in the final stages of the recruitment process and make a reasonable attempt to obtain information regarding any prior misconduct by the applicant within the last seven years. San Francisco State University is not currently sponsoring staff, management or faculty positions for H-1B employment visas. Advertised: September 02, 2026 (9:00 AM) Pacific Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App Send me jobs like these We will email you new jobs that match this search. Ok, we will send you jobs like this. The email address was invalid, please check for errors. You must agree to the privacy statement Subscribe Recaptcha Privacy agreement Search results Position Location Open Date Close Date Assistant Professor, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Special Education San Francisco September 02, 2026 San Francisco State University, Department of Special Education offers an exciting opportunity for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Mild to Moderate Support Needs (MMSN) beginning August 2027. Current opportunities Position Location Open Date Close Date Assistant Professor, Mild to Moderate Support Needs, Special Education San Francisco September 02, 2026 San Francisco State University, Department of Special Education offers an exciting opportunity for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Mild to Moderate Support Needs (MMSN) beginning August 2027. Temporary Hiring Pools Loading... Powered by PageUp Contact Email: hrwww@sfsu.edu Telephone: 415.338.1872 / Fax: 415.338.0521 Need Assistance? Location San Francisco State University 1600 Holloway Avenue, ADM 252 San Francisco, CA 94132 Office Hours Monday through Friday 8 a.m. - 5 p.m. Quick Links HR Client Support Center Calendar & Schedules FAQ's Forms SF State Gateway Accessibility Equity & Community Inclusion Environment & Sustainability Privacy Policy Title IX San Francisco State\n                            University A\n                            California State University Campus SF State Facebook SF State Twitter SF State Instagram SF State LinkedIn"
